#align(center, text(size: 20pt, weight: "bold")[Foundations Out of Sight])
#align(center, text(size: 13pt, style: "italic")[Darwin’s Concession and the Long Test of the Subsidence Theory of Coral Reefs, 1837–1960])
#v(1.2em)
#align(center)[#text(weight: "bold")[Abstract]]
Charles Darwin’s theory that atolls and barrier reefs grow upward on sinking foundations is often told as a theory read from the shapes of reefs and confirmed, a century later, by the drill. This report argues that the shape of an atoll was never the evidence on which the theory stood, and that Darwin said so. From 1842 he conceded that a reef indistinguishable from an atoll could form on any foundation that happened to lie at the right depth, and he rested subsidence on an argument from number: so many foundations could not all lie at that depth by chance. His principal critics, Karl Semper, John Murray, Alexander Agassiz and Reginald Daly, worked within that concession and attacked the premise, each supplying a way in which suitable foundations could be common without subsidence. Because form could not decide the question, and because the surface evidence each side offered could be reread by the other, the dispute passed, on Darwin’s own proposal, to boring. The verdict that drilling returned was narrower than the theory. The Funafuti borings of 1896–98 did not reach a foundation; the Eniwetok holes of 1952 did, and established that one atoll rests on a sunken volcano. That vindicated Darwin’s inference about foundations while leaving his sequence of reef forms an open question, which later work has treated separately.

== 1. Introduction
In May 1881 Darwin wrote to Alexander Agassiz about the latest attack on his theory of coral reefs. He closed with a wish: “I wish that some doubly rich millionaire would take it into his head to have borings made in some of the Pacific and Indian atolls, and bring home cores for slicing from a depth of 500 or 600 feet”.#footnote[Charles Darwin to Alexander Agassiz, 5 May 1881, in _The Life and Letters of Charles Darwin, Including an Autobiographical Chapter_, ed. Francis Darwin, vol. 3 (London: John Murray, 1887), 184.] Some years later a committee of the Royal Society bored into the atoll of Funafuti, under a preface that cited that letter as its warrant,#footnote[T. G. Bonney, preface to _The Atoll of Funafuti: Borings into a Coral Reef and the Results; Being the Report of the Coral Reef Committee of the Royal Society_ (London: Royal Society, 1904), vii and n. At vii: “the origin and history of a coral reef must remain uncertain until the experiment so earnestly desired by the late Charles Darwin himself had been made, and cores brought up for examination from a boring, which had been carried down to a depth of at least 600 feet”. At vii n: “‘Life and Letters,’ vol. 3, p. 184”. The preface’s footnote refers to the page of the letter quoted above.] and in 1960 two American geologists reported from Eniwetok, in the Marshall Islands: “Deep drilling revealed the presence of olivine basalt beneath shallow water limestone of Eocene age at a depth of more than 4,000 feet”.#footnote[Harry S. Ladd and Seymour O. Schlanger, _Drilling Operations on Eniwetok Atoll_, Geological Survey Professional Paper 260\-Y (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1960), 863.] Read in sequence, these three moments make a familiar story: a theory inferred from the shapes of reefs, contested for a century, and finally confirmed when the drill reached the volcano beneath.

This report argues that the familiar story misplaces the evidence. The shape of an atoll was not what Darwin’s case rested on, and he said as much. What he conceded, what his critics did with the concession, and why the question could be settled only by looking beneath a reef explain both why the controversy lasted as long as it did and why its resolution was narrower than the theory it is usually said to have confirmed. The thesis has three parts. First, Darwin conceded in 1842 that a reef like an atoll could grow on any foundation at the proper depth, so that form alone could not show subsidence; his inference rested instead on the improbability that so many foundations should lie at that depth. Second, his principal critics attacked that premise, by supplying mechanisms that would make suitable foundations common without subsidence, and the surface evidence that either side offered could be reread by the other. Third, the dispute therefore moved, on Darwin’s own proposal, to what lay beneath an atoll, and the drilling that answered it decided the question of foundations without deciding the question of forms.

The argument rests on printed sources: Charles Lyell’s _Principles of Geology_, the abstract of Darwin’s paper of 1837, the first and third editions of Darwin’s book on reefs, two of his letters, the works of Karl Semper, John Murray, James Dwight Dana, Alexander Agassiz and Reginald Daly, the Royal Society’s report on Funafuti, the Geological Survey’s report on the Eniwetok holes, and two recent papers. It is a reading of these texts, not a survey of the whole literature, and its limits are stated where they bear on the argument.

== 2. Reading the foundation from the shape
The first explanation of the atoll took its shape as evidence of what lay beneath it. In the second volume of the _Principles of Geology_, published in 1832, Lyell put it this way: “The circular or oval forms of the numerous coral isles of the Pacific, with the lagoons in their centre, naturally suggest the idea that they are nothing more than the crests of submarine volcanos, having the rims and bottoms of their craters overgrown by corals”.#footnote[Charles Lyell, _Principles of Geology, Being an Attempt to Explain the Former Changes of the Earth’s Surface, by Reference to Causes Now in Operation_, vol. 2 (London: John Murray, 1832), 290.] On this view the ring of reef repeated the ring of a crater: the foundation could be read from the form. Lyell anticipated the obvious objection, that many atolls were too large to be craters, and met it by appeal to the size of what were called craters of elevation.#footnote[Lyell, _Principles of Geology_, 2:291: “It may be objected that the circles formed by some coral reefs or groups of coral islets, varying as they do from ten to thirty miles and upwards in diameter, are so great as to preclude the idea of their being volcanic craters”. “In regard to this objection we may refer to what we have said in a former volume respecting the size of the so\-called craters of elevation”.]

Darwin’s paper read to the Geological Society on 31 May 1837, published as an abstract in the Society’s _Proceedings_, began by granting the crater theory its due. The abstract, written in the third person, reports Darwin’s view: “As long as Lagoon islands were considered the only difficulty to be solved, the belief that corals constructed their habitations (or speaking more correctly, their skeletons), on the crests of submarine craters, was both plausible and very ingenious”.#footnote[Charles Darwin, “On Certain Areas of Elevation and Subsidence in the Pacific and Indian Oceans, as Deduced from the Study of Coral Formations” (abstract of a paper read 31 May 1837), _Proceedings of the Geological Society of London_ 2, no. 51 (1837): 552.] What undid it was a second class of reef, the reef that encircles a high island at a distance. Of such reefs the abstract reports: “In this case it is impossible, on account of the nature of the central mass, to consider the reef as based on an external crater, or on any accumulation of sediment”.#footnote[Darwin, “Areas of Elevation and Subsidence,” 552.] A theory that explained atolls by the shape of their foundations had nothing to say about reefs whose foundations were plainly the slopes of ordinary islands.

In the book of 1842 Darwin pressed the objection from the side of form itself. The fifth chapter opens with the received view, “The theory most generally received on the formation of atolls, is that they are based on submarine craters”, and asks at once “where can we find a crater of the shape of Bow atoll, which is five times as long as it is broad”.#footnote[Charles Darwin, _The Structure and Distribution of Coral Reefs: Being the First Part of the Geology of the Voyage of the Beagle, under the Command of Capt. Fitzroy, R.N. during the Years 1832 to 1836_ (London: Smith, Elder, 1842), 88.] The shapes of atolls did not look like the shapes of craters. But the more important move in the chapter is not this objection. It is that Darwin stopped treating shape as evidence of foundation at all.

== 3. Darwin’s concession and the argument from number
Having objected to the crater theory, Darwin did not claim that an atoll could not rest on a crater. He wrote that “if the rim of a crater afforded a basis at the proper depth, I am far from denying that a reef like a perfectly characterized atoll might not be formed; some such, perhaps, now exist; but I cannot believe in the possibility of the greater number having thus originated”.#footnote[Darwin, _Structure and Distribution_ (1842), 89. Charles Darwin, _The Structure and Distribution of Coral Reefs_, 3rd ed., with an appendix by T. G. Bonney (London: Smith, Elder, 1889), 120. Both this edition and the third print “might not be formed”. Read strictly, the phrase after “far from denying” has one negative too many: the words that follow, “some such, perhaps, now exist”, show that Darwin is allowing that such a reef might be formed.] He made the same allowance for the rival theory of Chamisso, who had supposed that corals rising from a submarine base would reach the surface first at its edges and so form a ring: “if, therefore, corals were to grow up from a bank with a level surface some fathoms submerged, having steep sides and being situated in a deep sea, a reef not to be distinguished from an atoll, might be formed”.#footnote[Darwin, _Structure and Distribution_ (1842), 89: “he supposes that as the more massive kinds of corals prefer the surf, the outer portions, in a reef rising from a submarine basis, would first reach the surface and consequently form a ring”.] The allowance is a strong one. It grants that the characteristic form of an atoll, a ring of reef around a lagoon, could arise on a foundation that had never sunk. On Darwin’s own account, form did not distinguish the cases.

What did distinguish them, for Darwin, was number. Of Chamisso’s view he wrote in the same paragraph that a difficulty like the one facing the crater theory rendered it “inapplicable to the greater number of atolls”.#footnote[Darwin, _Structure and Distribution_ (1842), 89: “But a difficulty of the same kind with that affecting the crater theory, renders, as we shall presently see, this view inapplicable to the greater number of atolls”.] The difficulty is set out a few pages later: “A foundation must originally have been present beneath each atoll at that limited depth, which is indispensable for the first growth of the reef\-building polypifers”.#footnote[Darwin, _Structure and Distribution_ (1842), 92.] Across great areas of the Pacific and Indian Oceans in which no island rises higher than the waves can heap material,#footnote[Darwin, _Structure and Distribution_ (1842), 92: “enormous spaces, both in the Pacific and Indian oceans, are interspersed with islands, of which not one rises above that height, to which the waves and winds in an open sea can heap up matter”.] it was not credible that so many submarine summits should all stand within a few fathoms of the surface and none above it; nor could elevation have brought them there, since that supposition was just as improbable.#footnote[Darwin, _Structure and Distribution_ (1842), 93: “So highly improbable is this supposition, that we are compelled to believe, that the bases of the many atolls did never at any one period all lie submerged within the depth of a few fathoms beneath the surface, but that they were brought into the requisite position or level, some at one period and some at another, through movements in the earth’s crust”. “But this could not have been effected by elevation, for the belief that points so numerous and so widely\-separated were successively uplifted to a certain level, but that not one point was raised above that level, is quite as improbable as the former supposition”.] The conclusion followed: “If, then, the foundations of the many atolls were not uplifted into the requisite position, they must of necessity have subsided into it”.#footnote[Darwin, _Structure and Distribution_ (1842), 93–94.] In a footnote Darwin turned the same reasoning against Chamisso: “Chamisso’s theory, from assuming the existence of so many banks, all lying at the proper depth beneath the water, is also vitally defective”.#footnote[Darwin, _Structure and Distribution_ (1842), 94.]

The argument was not new in 1842. The abstract of 1837 already reports, among Darwin’s reasons for thinking general subsidence probable, “the extreme difficulty (with the knowledge that corals grow at but limited depths) in explaining the existence of a vast number of reefs on one level, without we grant subsidence”.#footnote[Darwin, “Areas of Elevation and Subsidence,” 553.] What the book added was an explicit statement of what the argument conceded. Its structure matters, because the rest of the controversy turned on it. Subsidence was not observed beneath any atoll. It was inferred by excluding the alternatives, and the exclusion depended on a premise about frequency: that foundations at the proper depth, without subsidence, would be rare. Anyone who could show that such foundations were common would leave the form of the atoll exactly as it was and remove the reason for thinking that it had sunk.

Two qualifications keep this reading from claiming too much. First, Darwin did not rest everything on the argument from number. The 1837 abstract reports that “within the lagoon of Keeling Island, proofs of subsidence might be deduced from many falling trees and a ruined storehouse”,#footnote[Darwin, “Areas of Elevation and Subsidence,” 553.] and the book proposed tests from the geographical distribution of reef types: whether the areas of atolls and barrier reefs stood in a determinate relation to those of fringing reefs and recent elevation, and whether recent volcanic action was connected with the movements so indicated. If such questions received answers in accordance with the theory, he wrote, then “not only is the theory confirmed”.#footnote[Darwin, _Structure and Distribution_ (1842), 116: “Do the areas which have subsided, as indicated by the presence of atolls and barrier\-reefs, and the areas which have remained stationary or have been upraised, as shown by fringing\-reefs, bear any determinate relation to each other”. “Is there any connection between the movements thus indicated, and recent volcanic action”. “if this can be satisfactorily shown, not only is the theory confirmed”.] The distribution argument was an independent line of evidence, and this report does not examine Darwin’s map. But it too was an inference from the surface, and it could be met in the same way, by finding reef types together where the theory said they should not be. Second, Darwin knew that a section through an atoll would record its history. In a footnote on the same page he set out to consider “the appearances, which would probably be presented by a vertical and deep section across a coral formation”, and described what such a section, formed during successive subsidences, ought to contain.#footnote[Darwin, _Structure and Distribution_ (1842), 116n: “I may take this opportunity of briefly considering the appearances, which would probably be presented by a vertical and deep section across a coral formation”. “This is a subject worthy of attention, as a means of comparison with ancient coral\-strata”.] In 1842 this was offered as a guide to recognising ancient reefs, not as a proposal to drill.

By the third edition of 1889 the concession stood, and the inference had hardened. The passage on craters now ends with the judgement that “it is incredible that the greater number could have thus originated”,#footnote[Darwin, _Structure and Distribution_ (1889), 120.] and near the close of the chapter Darwin wrote: “The necessity, also, that a foundation should have existed at the proper depth for the growth of the corals over certain large areas, almost compels us to accept this theory”.#footnote[Darwin, _Structure and Distribution_ (1889), 154.] It was the need for foundations, not the form of the reefs, that compelled assent.

== 4. The critics take up the premise
The criticisms of the following decades can be read as a series of attacks on the premise about frequency, joined to a growing doubt that the structure of a reef could show anything about vertical movement at all.

Karl Semper, whose investigations in the Pelew Islands had preceded Darwin’s second edition,#footnote[Alexander Agassiz, _The Coral Reefs of the Tropical Pacific_, Memoirs of the Museum of Comparative Zoölogy at Harvard College 28 (Cambridge, MA: Printed for the Museum, 1903), xiii: “As far back as 1861 he spent nearly ten months in the Pelews”. “Following Rein and Semper, recent investigators have, with few exceptions, regarded it as untenable”. Charles Darwin to Karl Semper, 2 October 1879, in _The Life and Letters of Charles Darwin, Including an Autobiographical Chapter_, ed. Francis Darwin, vol. 3 (London: John Murray, 1887), 182: “This is my sole and poor excuse for the mistake which I made in the second edition of my ‘Coral’ book”.] made the second point directly. Arguing against Dana’s version of the theory, he reasoned that if local upheavals could occur within regions of general subsidence, and the reverse, then “the structure of the reef itself can give no certain evidence whatever, as the basis of an assertion that this or that region is at a given moment undergoing upheaval or subsidence”.#footnote[Karl Semper, _Animal Life as Affected by the Natural Conditions of Existence_, International Scientific Series (New York: D. Appleton, 1881), 232: “if, as Dana says, all kinds of reefs may originate during subsidence, the structure of the reef itself is of no importance in investigating the question as to whether in any given spot subsidence or upheaval is taking place or has taken place”.] On one atoll he found large dead blocks lying above high water, and judged that their position “can only be regarded as proof of a recent upheaval”.#footnote[Semper, _Animal Life_, 239: “The position of these blocks, and their height above the highest water line, it seems to me can only be regarded as proof of a recent upheaval”.] When Semper sent Darwin the proof\-sheets of his account, Darwin replied in a letter later printed by his son.#footnote[Darwin to Semper, 2 October 1879, in _Life and Letters_, 3:182: “I thank you for your extremely kind letter of the 19th, and for the proof\-sheets”.] He admitted that he would have been troubled by what Semper found,#footnote[Darwin to Semper, 2 October 1879, in _Life and Letters_, 3:182: “But I should have been much troubled in my mind by the sea not being so deep as it usually is round atolls”.] and restated his concession in its sharpest form: “I always foresaw that a bank at the proper depth beneath the surface would give rise to a reef which could not be distinguished from an atoll, formed during subsidence”.#footnote[Darwin to Semper, 2 October 1879, in _Life and Letters_, 3:182.] He held to his conclusion for the central Pacific and Indian Oceans, but granted that “such cases as that of the Pellew Islands, if of at all frequent occurrence, would make my general conclusions of very little value. Future observers must decide between us”.#footnote[Darwin to Semper, 2 October 1879, in _Life and Letters_, 3:182: “I must still adhere to my opinion, that the atolls and barrier reefs in the middle of the Pacific and Indian Oceans indicate subsidence”.] The question, by Darwin’s own reckoning, had become one of frequency, to be settled by observations he could not himself make.

John Murray, one of the staff of the _Challenger_ expedition,#footnote[Francis Darwin, editorial note, in _The Life and Letters of Charles Darwin, Including an Autobiographical Chapter_, ed. Francis Darwin, vol. 3 (London: John Murray, 1887), 183: “Mr. Murray, one of the staff of the _Challenger_”.] attacked the premise head on. His paper to the Royal Society of Edinburgh in April 1880, published as an abstract, set out to show that “there are other agencies at work in the tropical oceanic regions by which submarine elevations can be built up from very great depths so as to form a foundation for coral reefs”.#footnote[John Murray, “On the Structure and Origin of Coral Reefs and Islands” (abstract of a paper read 5 April 1880), _Proceedings of the Royal Society of Edinburgh_ 10, no. 107 (1879–80): 506: “during his examination of the coral reefs in the Pelew group, experienced great difficulties in applying Darwin’s theory”.] Turning to Darwin’s own discussion of submerged banks, he observed: “However, the improbability of so many submerged banks existing in the open ocean caused him to reject this mode of formation for atolls”. The deep\-sea work of the _Challenger_, he argued, removed the improbability, since it showed “that submerged banks are continually in process of formation in the tropical regions of the ocean”.#footnote[Murray, “Structure and Origin,” 510–11. At 511: “recent deep\-sea investigations have shown that submerged banks are continually in process of formation in the tropical regions of the ocean, and it is in a high degree probable that the majority of atolls are seated on banks formed in this manner”.] The lagoon he explained by vigorous growth at the margin and the solution of dead coral within, and he concluded that subsidence was not needed to explain any characteristic feature of barrier reefs or atolls.#footnote[Murray, “Structure and Origin,” 517: “That when coral plantations build up from submarine banks they assume an atoll form, owing to the more abundant supply of food to the outer margins, and the removal of dead coral rock from the interior portions by currents and by the action of the carbonic acid dissolved in sea\-water”. “That it is not necessary to call in subsidence to explain any of the characteristic features of barrier reefs or atolls, and that all these features would exist alike in areas of slow elevation, of rest, or of slow subsidence”.] Murray did not deny that atolls looked as Darwin described. He denied the frequency premise, on new evidence about the deep sea, and so removed the inference while leaving the form untouched.

Darwin saw the point at once. Writing to Agassiz in May 1881, he repeated the concession and the premise together, insisting that he could “hardly believe in the former presence of as many banks (there having been no subsidence) as there are atolls in the great oceans”.#footnote[Darwin to Agassiz, 5 May 1881, in _Life and Letters_, 3:184: “I have expressly said that a bank at the proper depth would give rise to an atoll, which could not be distinguished from one formed during subsidence. I can, however, hardly believe in the former presence of as many banks (there having been no subsidence) as there are atolls in the great oceans, within a reasonable depth, on which minute oceanic organisms could have accumulated to the thickness of many hundred feet”.] His reply to Murray was not an argument from the shape of reefs. It was a reassertion of the premise, now about the thickness of material that Murray’s organisms would have had to supply, and it ended with the wish for borings.

Dana, to whom Agassiz credited the general acceptance of the theory,#footnote[Agassiz, _Coral Reefs of the Tropical Pacific_, xiii: “To Professor Dana was undoubtedly due the general acceptance of Darwin’s theory of the formation of coral reefs”.] answered the same challenge on the same ground. Of reports that reef rock in the Solomon Islands lay over limestone full of pelagic organisms he wrote: “They are not evidence against the subsidence theory, but simply local examples under the general principle just stated”.#footnote[James D. Dana, _Corals and Coral Islands_, 3rd ed. (New York: Dodd, Mead, 1890), 291: “In support of this view, Dr. Guppy states that in elevated coral reefs of the Solomon Islands, one hundred to twelve hundred feet high, the coral reef rock forms a comparatively thin layer over impure earthy limestone abounding in foraminifers, pteropods, and other pelagic organisms”.] He denied the frequency Murray needed: “The wide oceans are, however, wonderfully free from banks approaching the surface within one hundred fathoms”.#footnote[Dana, _Corals and Coral Islands_, 292.] Of Agassiz’s view that atolls in the West Indies stood on a basement of pelagic limestone he wrote: “It may be so; but there is as yet no proof of it”.#footnote[Dana, _Corals and Coral Islands_, 292: “The atoll of the Tortugas, and others in the West Indies, are regarded by Mr. Agassiz as having a basement, up to the coral\-growing limit, of pelagic limestone or of some other material”.] Dana also offered evidence of another kind, which is the strongest counter\-instance to the reading proposed here. In the deep, fiord\-like bays of high islands within barrier reefs he saw “not merely probable but positive evidence of subsidence”: drowned valleys, visible above water, recording that the land had sunk.#footnote[Dana, _Corals and Coral Islands_, 273: “There is, further, not merely probable but positive evidence of subsidence in the deep coast\-indentations of the high islands within the great barriers. The long points and deep fiord\-like bays are such as exist only where a land, after having been deeply gouged by erosion, has become half submerged”.] Here, at least for barrier reefs, was surface evidence of subsidence that did not depend on frequency. It did not settle the matter. In 1915 Daly granted that some embayments might be explained in Dana’s way,#footnote[Reginald A. Daly, “The Glacial\-Control Theory of Coral Reefs,” _Proceedings of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences_ 51, no. 4 (1915): 224: “Dana regarded such valleys as proofs of crustal subsidence and Davis has adopted the same view. Some of the island embayments may be correctly explained in this way”.] but argued from coastlines elsewhere for “a Recent, general rising of the ocean's surface”.#footnote[Daly, “Glacial\-Control Theory,” 224: “the more unavoidable is the hypothesis that most of the world's drowned valleys were submerged because of a Recent, general rising of the ocean's surface”.] A drowned valley showed that the sea now stood higher against the land; by itself it did not show which of the two had moved. And for atolls, where no land remained above water, the evidence was not available at all.

Agassiz’s survey of the tropical Pacific, published in 1903, summed up the critics’ position: “A number of explorers in recent years have attacked limited areas, and their conclusions have in nearly all instances been opposed to Darwin’s theory”.#footnote[Agassiz, _Coral Reefs of the Tropical Pacific_, xiv.] His alternative foundations were platforms cut by erosion and solution from elevated volcanic and limestone masses,#footnote[Agassiz, _Coral Reefs of the Tropical Pacific_, xx: “The erosive as well as the solvent action of the sea would soon level the outer rim to the plane of the sea, the further disintegration being stopped by the growth of recent corals or of coralline Algæ upon the surface of the coralliferous or massive limestone eroded to the level of the sea or below it”.] and he turned Dana’s reasoning about local foundations back on him: where such rock bases occur, “we may justly reply that they have great interest as they are opposed to the application of the theory of subsidence as explanatory of the formation of coral reefs in the areas where these rock bases occur”.#footnote[Agassiz, _Coral Reefs of the Tropical Pacific_, xx n.] For the low atolls that mattered most to Darwin’s argument, the legend to Agassiz’s own reef chart conceded the decisive gap. It described them as low atolls whose land rims were of modern reef rock or conglomerate, “the underlying base of which is not known”.#footnote[Agassiz, _Coral Reefs of the Tropical Pacific_, xxxi: “Islands and groups colored yellow indicate low atolls elevated perhaps a few feet, but of which the land rim consists of modern reef rock or conglomerate or breccia, the underlying base of which is not known”.]

Daly’s glacial\-control theory completed the series by supplying foundations at the right depth everywhere at once. Platforms cut by the waves while sea level was lowered in the Pleistocene were colonised by corals as the sea rose again.#footnote[Daly, “Glacial\-Control Theory,” 159: “The depth of the platforms, cut\-and\-built by the waves during maximum glaciation, was estimated to be from 30 to 50 fathoms, or 55 to 90 m., below present sea\-level”. “The warming of the tropical seas allowed the coral larvae, emanating from the limited reefs not entirely killed in spite of the Pleistocene chilling, to colonize the new, wave\-cut platforms”.] In his summary: “The fringing, barrier, and atoll reefs are thus explained as shallow crowns recently built up on wave\-formed platforms”.#footnote[Daly, “Glacial\-Control Theory,” 159.] He stated the contrast plainly: “The offered explanation does not involve any vertical movements of the earth's crust and thus contrasts with the famous Darwin\-Dana theory”.#footnote[Daly, “Glacial\-Control Theory,” 159.] Daly’s mechanism answered Darwin’s premise in its most general form, since a single cause brought many platforms to a common level within the reach of corals. He drew the corresponding conclusion about form: “The series of topographic forms, from fringing reefs through barrier reefs to atoll reefs, look as simple as a natural sequence can well be, but it may be wholly deceptive”.#footnote[Daly, “Glacial\-Control Theory,” 245.]

It would misrepresent the period to suggest that the critics carried the day. Reviewing the controversy in an appendix to the third edition of Darwin’s book in 1889, T. G. Bonney judged it premature to regard the theory as disproved, and concluded that “as the evidence at present stands, it is insufficient to justify a decision adverse to Mr. Darwin's theory as a general explanation”.#footnote[T. G. Bonney, “Summary of the Principal Contributions to the History of Coral\-Reefs since the Year 1874,” appendix 2 in Charles Darwin, _The Structure and Distribution of Coral Reefs_, 3rd ed. (London: Smith, Elder, 1889), 332: “It is then, I think, premature to regard the theory which was advanced by Mr. Darwin, and has received the approval of an observer of such an exceptional experience as Professor Dana, as conclusively disproved by the results of the more recent investigations”.] The point here is not who was winning but what the dispute had become. By the 1880s both sides accepted that the form of a reef did not reveal its foundation; they disagreed about how often suitable foundations could arise without subsidence; and each surface criterion offered in evidence, from raised blocks to drowned valleys, admitted a second reading. The question had moved out of sight, beneath the reef.

== 5. The test beneath the reef
Darwin had named the test in his letter of 1881. Agassiz named it too, at the end of the introduction to his survey: “Most important is an extensive system of boring at well\-selected points”.#footnote[Agassiz, _Coral Reefs of the Tropical Pacific_, xxxii.] The Royal Society’s Coral Reef Committee, which the title page of its report lists with Bonney as chairman, framed its work at Funafuti in the same terms.#footnote[The title page of the report lists the members of the committee and names Bonney as its chairman.] Bonney’s preface to its report recalls the conviction that “the origin and history of a coral reef must remain uncertain until the experiment so earnestly desired by the late Charles Darwin himself had been made, and cores brought up for examination from a boring, which had been carried down to a depth of at least 600 feet”.#footnote[Bonney, preface to _Atoll of Funafuti_, vii.] Edgeworth David, narrating the second expedition, opened his account of its results with that request: “The depth for which Darwin had asked that a coral atoll should be bored with a view to testing his theory (500–600 feet) had been exceeded by reaching 698 feet”.#footnote[T. W. Edgeworth David, “Narrative of the Second and Third Expeditions,” in _The Atoll of Funafuti_ (London: Royal Society, 1904), 54.] The work did not stop there; the preface records that “the submarine contour of the island, determined by Captain Field’s soundings, suggested that the base of the coral reef might be very near, so the Boring was continued in 1898, as narrated in Section IV, until a depth of 1114 feet had been reached”.#footnote[Bonney, preface to _Atoll of Funafuti_, xii.]

Funafuti met the letter of Darwin’s request and missed its purpose. The lowest part of the core had at first looked like the beginning of the original foundation, but proved on examination to be reef material converted to dolomite; David concluded: “It was evident then, that the base of the coral\-reef rock had not yet been attained”.#footnote[David, “Narrative of the Second and Third Expeditions,” 54: “At first sight it seemed like a consolidated chalky ooze, and our first impression was that it represented the beginning of the original foundation of the coral atoll. Later examination in the laboratories at the Royal College of Science, London (for the results of which see Sections XII. and XIV.), and at Sydney University, showed that this rock was largely coral\-reef material converted into dolomite. It was evident then, that the base of the coral\-reef rock had not yet been attained”.] Without a foundation, the question whether one had sunk could be approached only indirectly, through the depths at which the organisms in the core had lived, and contributors to the report did approach it so. David and his colleagues noted that “it might be argued that an absence of pteropod shells from the core at a depth as great as 186 fathoms (the maximum depth attained by the bore) argues subsidence”;#footnote[T. W. Edgeworth David, G. H. Halligan, and A. E. Finckh, “Report on Dredging at Funafuti,” in _The Atoll of Funafuti_ (London: Royal Society, 1904), 156.] J. W. Judd, estimating the rate at which the alga _Halimeda_ was accumulating on the floor of the lagoon, concluded that “a subsidence of 25 fathoms (150 feet) must have taken place in this 800 years”.#footnote[J. W. Judd, “General Report on the Materials Sent from Funafuti, and the Methods of Dealing with Them,” in _The Atoll of Funafuti_ (London: Royal Society, 1904), 180: “But it is possibly not an excessive estimate to take 1 inch per annum as the rate of accumulation of the broken fronds of _Halimeda_ on the lagoon floor”.] These inferences show that Funafuti was not silent on subsidence. But they were of the same indirect kind that had been in dispute for forty years, resting on assumed depths of growth and assumed rates of accumulation, and Bonney’s preface declined to draw them together: “Into the controversies about the development of coral reefs, those who have been concerned in the preparation of this volume have not attempted to enter. They have endeavoured to state facts and leave it to readers to interpret these for themselves”.#footnote[Bonney, preface to _Atoll of Funafuti_, xiii.]

The critics read the same rock differently. Agassiz held that below the modern reef rock the bore must have “penetrated either an underlying mass of tertiary limestone similar to that of the raised tertiary limestones of Tonga, Fiji, Niue, Nauru, and Paanopa, or have passed through the mass of recent reef rock forming the outer talus of the atoll of Funafuti”,#footnote[Agassiz, _Coral Reefs of the Tropical Pacific_, xxii.] and he proposed that the alternating beds of a reef flat, built up and cut down by the sea, might explain “the successive layers of rock met with by boring through the upper strata at Funafuti, said to have been due to subsidence”.#footnote[Agassiz, _Coral Reefs of the Tropical Pacific_, 117: “the existence of alternate layers of beach rock and of recent conglomerate or of coral rubble does not by any means indicate a talus; it merely indicates a reef flat over which the sea has either been building up beaches, or cutting down ledges, or adding masses of coral sand or shingle”. “This may explain much of the character of the successive layers of rock met with by boring through the upper strata at Funafuti, said to have been due to subsidence”.] Daly argued that if a reef grows outward over its own talus as its foundation sinks, a bore near the rim would pass through talus rather than reef in place,#footnote[Daly, “Glacial\-Control Theory,” 247: “the massive reef of a large atoll must lie unconformably upon talus of indefinite depth. Hence the Funafuti borings could not, in any case, have penetrated massive reef material _in situ_ to a depth greater than about 45 m”.] and wrote: “That the actual site of the borings was unwisely chosen is apparent”. The theory needed testing by bore\-holes “sunk well inside the reefs of atolls and barrier\-encircled islands”.#footnote[Daly, “Glacial\-Control Theory,” 247.] He added a point against Darwin that a fair account must record: the boring at Bermuda, which he called the nearest approach to a vital test, did not favour subsidence, though he held that its value must remain subordinate until other borings were made in the Pacific and Indian Oceans.#footnote[Daly, “Glacial\-Control Theory,” 247–48: “The Bermuda boring gives the nearest approach to a vital test. Its result does not favor the subsidence theory but its value must remain subordinate until other borings are made at properly selected points on other platforms, preferably those in the Pacific and Indian oceans”.] The Funafuti boring had produced more than a thousand feet of core that each party could read in its own favour, because it had not reached the one thing that all parties agreed would decide the case.

== 6. What Eniwetok decided
The deep holes at Eniwetok, drilled in connection with tests carried out in the Marshall Islands by the Atomic Energy Commission,#footnote[Ladd and Schlanger, _Drilling Operations on Eniwetok Atoll_, 863: “In connection with tests carried out in the Marshall Islands by the Atomic Energy Commission and the Los Alamos Scientific Laboratory, considerable drilling was done on Eniwetok Atoll”.] reached it. Harry Ladd and Seymour Schlanger, reporting for the Geological Survey in 1960, stated the result without hedging: “The deep drilling on Eniwetok, however, firmly established the fact that the foundation of that atoll is a basaltic volcano that rises 2 miles above the floor of the ocean”.#footnote[Ladd and Schlanger, _Drilling Operations on Eniwetok Atoll_, 899.] They counted this among the most important “features of Darwin's subsidence theory, which postulated that atolls may develop on the tops of slowly sinking mountains”.#footnote[Ladd and Schlanger, _Drilling Operations on Eniwetok Atoll_, 899.] The character of the first sediments was decisive in exactly the sense that the earlier controversy required: “The first sediments to be laid down were Eocene in age, and they contained reef corals that do not flourish below 150 feet”.#footnote[Ladd and Schlanger, _Drilling Operations on Eniwetok Atoll_, 899.] Reef rock formed in shallow water, resting on a volcano that now lies more than four thousand feet down, is the observation for which Darwin’s argument from number had stood in. It is also an observation that pelagic banks built up from great depths, platforms eroded at sea level without later sinking, or benches cut by the Pleistocene sea would not lead one to expect.

Three features of the result keep it from being the vindication of the whole theory that it is often taken to be. First, it concerned one atoll. The drilling established the foundation of Eniwetok, not of atolls in general; Darwin’s argument had been about the many, and a drill reaches one at a time. Second, the history it revealed was not a steady sinking. Leached zones, land shells and pollen in the section showed that “the overall history of subsidence was interrupted by periods of emergence”.#footnote[Ladd and Schlanger, _Drilling Operations on Eniwetok Atoll_, 900: “In both places there appear to have been times when the tops of the atolls stood above the sea for appreciable lengths of time and the shells and skeletons of aragonite\-secreting organisms were largely leached or recrystallized to calcite”. “These several types of evidence indicate that at both Eniwetok and Bikini the overall history of subsidence was interrupted by periods of emergence”.] And the two deepest holes differed below 1,400 feet; the thick beds of the northwestern hole, the authors judged, “were deposited on an outer slope in waters appreciably deeper than those to the southeast”.#footnote[Ladd and Schlanger, _Drilling Operations on Eniwetok Atoll_, 899–900. At 899: “Below 1,400 feet in the two deepest holes there were conspicuous differences in lithologic characters”. At 900: “It is probable, as Todd and Low suggest, that the _Globigerina_\-rich sediments of the northwest were deposited on an outer slope in waters appreciably deeper than those to the southeast”.] Third, and most important for the thesis of this report, the drilling answered a question about foundations, not about forms. It showed that the Eniwetok volcano had sunk while reef grew on it. It did not show that the reef had passed through fringing and barrier stages on the way.

Recent work draws the same line. Blanchon and his colleagues, writing in 2014, put it directly: “Although data from drilling confirmed Darwin’s postulate that atolls had subsided, they were insufficient to confirm the link between reef types”.#footnote[Paul Blanchon et al., “Postglacial Fringing\-Reef to Barrier\-Reef Conversion on Tahiti Links Darwin’s Reef Types,” _Scientific Reports_ 4 (2014): 4997, 1, #link("https://doi.org/10.1038/srep04997").] The drilling record, they add, shows that atolls “are not formed during a single episode of vertical reef growth but have complex thermotectonic and eustatic histories”.#footnote[Blanchon et al., “Postglacial Fringing\-Reef to Barrier\-Reef Conversion,” 1: “Clearly, atolls are not formed during a single episode of vertical reef growth but have complex thermotectonic and eustatic histories that reflect their long plate\-tectonic migration”.] Their own cores from Tahiti, where Darwin’s hypothesis first took shape,#footnote[Blanchon et al., “Postglacial Fringing\-Reef to Barrier\-Reef Conversion,” 1: “Darwin’s coral\-reef hypothesis, that fringing reefs transform first into barrier reefs and then atolls during subsidence, came into focus in November 1835 when HMS Beagle visited the South Pacific island of Tahiti”.] show that “a fringing reef retreated upslope during postglacial sea\-level rise and transformed into a barrier reef when it encountered a Pleistocene reef\-flat platform”: a link between reef types, but by a mechanism of rising sea level that Darwin did not propose.#footnote[Blanchon et al., “Postglacial Fringing\-Reef to Barrier\-Reef Conversion,” 1.] A review of atoll ecology published in 2024 gives Darwin the foundation and withholds the form: “Darwin correctly recognized that atolls are linked to the remnants of submerged volcanoes, around which coral reefs grew upward against the steady subsidence of the volcano”; but “Darwin’s subsidence model overlooks that the appearance of atolls today in their rim\-island configuration is merely a transient stage of longer geo\-ecological cycles”.#footnote[Sebastian Steibl et al., “A Renaissance of Atoll Ecology,” _Annual Review of Ecology, Evolution, and Systematics_ 55 (2024): 303, #link("https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-ecolsys-102722-013447"). The second passage cites a 2021 review by Droxler and Jorry, which is not examined here.]

== 7. Conclusion
The subsidence theory of coral reefs is often presented as a deduction from the shapes of reefs that waited a century for confirmation. The sources examined here support a different description. Darwin conceded that the shape of an atoll could not show what lay beneath it, and he rested subsidence on the improbability that so many foundations should lie at the proper depth. His critics did not refute his reading of reef forms; they attacked that premise, each with a new way of making suitable foundations common, while the surface evidence offered on either side proved open to a second reading. The controversy was long because its decisive object was out of sight, and it ended, for the question it could settle, when a drill reached a volcano under Eniwetok.

What it settled was narrower than the theory. Eniwetok vindicated Darwin’s inference about foundations, for one atoll, with an interrupted rather than a steady subsidence. It did not vindicate the sequence of reef forms, which the recent literature treats as a separate problem, reopened and in part answered by mechanisms tied to changes of sea level. On this reading the history of the controversy is less a story of a theory confirmed than of a question clarified: the drill showed which part of Darwin’s theory had depended on what could not be seen.

The argument has limits. It rests on a selection of printed sources and does not examine Darwin’s coloured map, Dana’s fuller treatment of distribution, the later borings that Daly and others called for, or the geological reviews on which the 2024 survey draws; any of these could qualify the claim that surface evidence was always open to a second reading. Two of its key texts, Darwin’s paper of 1837 and Murray’s of 1880, are available here only as published abstracts, the first in the third person. And it treats Funafuti as undecided on the question of its foundation, while acknowledging that several of its contributors drew inferences for subsidence from the organisms in the core.

